# Indexer speed: term generation and assembly dominate build time

Measured on an internal corpus (~46,732 documents; figures indicative):
per-document term generation and document assembly is ~94-95% of the
build phase (~145 µs/doc gene, ~210 µs/doc phenotype); canonical
encoding is ~1.3% and the SHA-256 change digest ~2.7-3.6%. The build
cost lives in term generation, not in the change hash, so the
incremental sidecar keeps a SHA-256 digest.

Static finding: the code constructs a fresh TermGenerator, an English
Snowball Stem, and a SimpleStopper for every document (several heap
allocations per document), and re-tokenises and re-stems the same short
strings (dataset, species, group, tissue) across thousands of
documents. The absolute cost of the per-document construction is not
yet measured.

Plan (risk-ordered; every lever gated by document-for-document parity):

1. Sub-profile the term-generation block (termgen/stem/stopper
   construction, index_text with and without positions, prefixed terms,
   RDF cache lookups, docdata assembly, value slots) to locate the
   dominant sub-cost before choosing any lever.
2. L1a (low risk): build the Stem once and lend it; keep a fresh
   TermGenerator per document so term positions stay correct.
3. L4 (low risk): reduce docdata string copies; serialized bytes
   unchanged.
4. L1b (deferred; needs a vendored xapian-rs shim to reset term
   positions): also reuse the TermGenerator.
5. L2/L3 (deferred; high risk, separate workstream): memoise tokenisation
   and stemming; must reproduce index_text semantics byte-for-byte.

Gate for every lever: the twin-index comparison (identical document
data, terms with wdf and sorted positions, value slots) stays clean,
and a lever whose measured win lands inside the run-to-run noise is
reverted rather than shipped.

